Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.809 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  two years ago on her birthday a friend  shared something I will never forget for  her privacy I'll call her Emma Emma  shared I'm incredibly blessed and  thankful to evade it to 30 years old I  don't know much about my kidney donor  except for his cause of death and that  he and I were the same age 29 no one  prepares you for the rollercoaster of  emotions a pure excitement and guilt you  feel when we're saving an organ donation  today on my birthday  all I can do is pray for him I promise I  will never forget what a gift you have  given me organ donation is a tough topic  not only is it immensely personal but we  have to talk about death something very  few of us like to think about let alone  discuss but we need to have more of  these conversations the more we can  discuss death an organ donation the more  likely we are to register as donors and  it's imperative that we have more donors  over a hundred and fifteen thousand  people in America are on transplant wait  lists it is not a fun place to be your  health is deteriorating while you're  hoping for a donated organ every time  the phone rings there's a rush of  adrenaline is there finally an organ for  me for thousands time runs out before  that phone call comes what's shocking is  that the likelihood of getting that  phone call and receiving an organ  transplant vary significantly by region  organs are viable outside of the body  for only a short window of time so the  organ donation network is set up  regionally when an organ becomes  available it's offered to the person at  the top of the waitlist in that region  this means people who live in areas with  lots of donors have a much  a chance at a new life than those who  live in areas with few donors here in  California we have some of the best  hospitals in the world but it is not the  best place if you need an organ  transplant waitlist are some of the  longest in the country this is why Steve  Jobs got his liver transplant in  Tennessee at the time the wait list for  a liver in Tennessee was under six  months while the national average was  over a year and some areas had wait  lists over three years people are  allowed to relocate to capitalize on  shorter wait lists but very few people  have the resources time or health to do  so the vast majority are on the wait  list at the transplant center closest to  them in California and across the  country we simply don't have enough  organs to meet the demand less than 54  percent of US residents here in  California are registered as donors this  is below the national average and well  below regions like Montana and Alaska  where over 80 percent of the population  is registered underlying these  geographical differences are differences  by race and recency of immigration  minorities and people who have emigrated  recently tend to donate less frequently  so populations like California that are  more heterogeneous tend to have lower  donation rates this is particularly  concerning because to get a transplant  you not only to get to the top of the  waitlist but you have to match with the  donated organ based on blood type tissue  biomarkers and size many of these  characteristics are correlated to race  so matches are much more likely to be  found within our own respective ethnic  and racial groups our geographic and  racial communities need us by being an  organ donor we have the opportunity to  go on and save up to 8p  lives these people might be a burn  victim who needs a skin transplant a  neighbor with type 1 diabetes who needs  a kidney or pancreas or a classmate with  a severe viral infection who needs a new  heart one act of generosity can go a  very long way as you consider being a  donor I'd encourage you to have two  conversations one with yourself and one  with your loved ones the conversation  with yourself make sure your questions  are answered being an organ donor is an  amazing opportunity but it can be  unnerving and for many of us the first  time we consider being an organ donor is  at the DMV of all places  who feels introspective and charitable  after waiting in line for two hours when  I was first rung the question of being  an organ donor at the DMV  I checked the box No  at 17 I couldn't shake that doctors  wouldn't work as hard to save me if they  knew my organs could be used elsewhere I  had yet to get the reassurance that the  priority of the health care system is to  save our lives where there were donors  or not and that the doctors treating us  don't even have access to the donor  registry that's all managed by an  external nonprofit my situation was  probably not unique we need to have  these conversations with ourselves on  our own time when we can get the  reassurances we need we can then go and  register at the DMV or online at organ  donor gov I'm guessing many of you are  wondering why add this personal  conversation now I'm young and don't  plan to die anytime soon I surely hope  that's the case for all of us in the  room but I'd argue that it's a lot  easier to think about mortality now when  it feels a long way off and to get  comfort  with our decisions being young gave me  the opportunity to reconsider my initial  no and as of last year makes me a proud  yes the second conversation is probably  the tougher one the conversation with  our significant others and families if  we're ever in a situation where our  organs can be donated  we'll be dying and our families will be  having one of the toughest days of their  lives we can make this a little bit  easier on them by clarifying our desires  in advance this is incredibly important  hundreds of registered donors don't get  the opportunity to donate their organs  because their families insist otherwise  at the hospital we need to prepare them  to make the decisions we want similarly  let's ask them what they want for  themselves let's ask them what questions  they have and help them answer them and  make sure we are prepared to facilitate  their wishes if needed I find organ  donation one of the most humbling and  inspiring statements of human kindness  one person giving the gift of life to  others like Emma I hope that we all have  long and healthy lives  but unpredictable situations happen have  the conversation with yourself have the  conversation with your loved ones and  let's step up and be there for our  communities thank you  [Applause]  you

